@startuml signalr-hub-flow
title Kubernetes + oauth2-proxy + Okta\nSecuring a SignalR Hub (negotiate + WebSocket upgrade through the proxy)

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center

actor User
participant "Browser\n(Angular SPA\n@microsoft/signalr)" as B
box "Kubernetes cluster" #F7F7F7
    participant "Ingress\n(routes app.example.com\nto oauth2-proxy Service)" as I
    participant "oauth2-proxy\nDeployment/Service\n(--proxy-websockets=true)" as P
    participant "ASP.NET Core API\nDeployment/Service\n([Authorize] NotificationsHub)" as A
end box
participant "Okta\n(OIDC provider)" as O

note over B, P
  Precondition: the user already signed in via the standard OIDC
  authorization-code flow, so the browser holds only the encrypted
  HttpOnly _oauth2_proxy cookie. JavaScript has no token.
end note

== 1. Negotiate (ordinary HTTP POST) ==

User -> B: App starts the hub connection
B -> B: new HubConnectionBuilder()\n.withUrl('/api/hubs/notifications')\n.withAutomaticReconnect().build()
B -> I: POST /api/hubs/notifications/negotiate?negotiateVersion=1\nCookie: _oauth2_proxy=...
I -> P: Forward
P -> P: Decrypt/validate session cookie,\nload the stored Okta ID token
P -> A: POST .../negotiate\nAuthorization: Bearer <okta-id-token>
note right of P
  Cookie -> Token pass-through
  (--pass-authorization-header=true)
  is transport-agnostic: it applies to
  the negotiate call and to the
  WebSocket handshake alike.
end note
A -> A: JwtBearer validates the token\n(issuer, audience, signature, expiry)\n-> [Authorize] on the Hub passes
A --> P: 200 { connectionId, connectionToken,\n  availableTransports: [WebSockets, SSE, LongPolling] }
P --> B: 200 (same body)

== 2. WebSocket handshake ==

B -> I: GET /api/hubs/notifications?id=<connectionToken>\nUpgrade: websocket, Connection: Upgrade\nCookie: _oauth2_proxy=...
note right of B
  The browser cannot set an Authorization
  header on a WebSocket handshake - but it
  does not need to. It sends the cookie
  automatically (same-origin), and the proxy
  supplies the bearer token upstream.
  No ?access_token= query string, and no
  OnMessageReceived event handler.
end note
I -> P: Forward (upgrade headers preserved)
P -> P: Decrypt cookie, load session
P -> A: GET .../hubs/notifications (upgrade)\nAuthorization: Bearer <okta-id-token>
A -> A: JwtBearer validates the token;\nHubCallerContext.User populated,\nContext.UserIdentifier = "sub"
A --> P: 101 Switching Protocols
P --> B: 101 Switching Protocols
A -> A: NotificationsHub.OnConnectedAsync()\nGroups.AddToGroupAsync(ConnectionId, tenantId)

== 3. Live connection ==

B -> A: invoke("Subscribe", topic) over the open socket
A --> B: Clients.Caller / Clients.User(userId) / Clients.Group(...)
note over B, A
  Once upgraded, frames flow through the proxy without
  re-authentication - the cookie and the bearer token are
  checked at handshake time only.
end note

== 4. Expiry and reconnect ==

A -> A: Bearer token exp reached
note right of A
  MapHub(..., o => o.CloseOnAuthenticationExpiration = true)
  makes SignalR close the connection instead of letting an
  authenticated socket outlive its token.
end note
A --> B: Close frame (authentication expired)
B -> I: Automatic reconnect: POST .../negotiate\nCookie: _oauth2_proxy=...
I -> P: Forward
alt Session cookie still valid (--cookie-refresh keeps it fresh)
    P -> O: Refresh token grant (if the ID token expired)
    O --> P: New ID token
    P -> A: POST .../negotiate\nAuthorization: Bearer <new-id-token>
    A --> B: 200 -> handshake repeats, connection restored
else Session expired
    P --> B: 302 Location: https://{okta-domain}/oauth2/default/v1/authorize?...
    note right of B
      XHR/WebSocket cannot complete an interactive
      redirect. The SignalR client surfaces a failed
      reconnect; the SPA should reload the page so the
      browser follows the redirect and signs in again.
    end note
end

@enduml
